// ARC-SEC-6 — a secret is compared in a timing-resistant way.
//
// `===` returns as soon as two bytes differ, so the time it takes leaks how
// much of the guess was right. That is enough to recover a token one character
// at a time, and nothing about the code looks wrong while it happens.
//
// Matched on the member's name rather than by a regex over the whole
// comparison: Grit's regex binds capture groups to variables, so a grouped
// alternation errors instead of matching. Verified against the fixtures.
`$object.$field === $other` where {
  // `code` alone is excluded on purpose: an error code is compared with
  // `===` legitimately and constantly (ARC-ERR-6 requires exactly that), so
  // including it would make the rule fire on correct code more often than on
  // a secret. A secret's field says what it is.
  $field <: or { `token`, `password`, `secret`, `hash`, `otpCode`, `accessToken`, `refreshToken` },
  register_diagnostic(
    span = $field,
    message = "ARC-SEC-6: compare a secret with a timing-safe function, never with ===."
  )
}
